The construction, however, uses only two properties of the score, and it is worth stating them exactly.
Let 𝒮 \mathcal{S} carry a strict order > > and let J : ℕ → 𝒮 J:\mathbb{N}\to\mathcal{S} satisfy: (i) extensionality : J ⁡ ( p ) J(p) depends only on the partial function φ p \varphi_{p} , not on the program text (so we may write J J on partial computable functions); and (ii) attainable comparability : there exist partial computable functions ψ lo , ψ hi \psi_{\mathrm{lo}},\psi_{\mathrm{hi}} with J ⁡ ( ψ lo ) < J ⁡ ( ψ hi ) J(\psi_{\mathrm{lo}})<J(\psi_{\mathrm{hi}}) .
Then no total computable certifier is sound and complete for strict improvement with respect to J J :
fix a base index p p with φ p = ψ lo \varphi_{p}=\psi_{\mathrm{lo}} , and let f ⁡ ( e ) f(e) be an index of the program that first computes V ⁡ ( p , e ) V(p,e) and then simulates a fixed program for ψ lo \psi_{\mathrm{lo}} if V ⁡ ( p , e ) = 1 V(p,e)=1 and one for ψ hi \psi_{\mathrm{hi}} otherwise; the argument of Theorem 1 goes through verbatim.
Each hypothesis enters at exactly one point: extensionality makes the flip meaningful (the two branches realize the two J J -values);
attainable comparability is the non-triviality requirement (note that non-constancy alone does not suffice if > > is a partial order — the two attained values must be comparable — and that for a constant J J the certifier V ≡ 0 V\equiv 0 is trivially sound and complete);
the order on 𝒮 \mathcal{S} enters only through the single comparison J ⁡ ( ψ hi ) > J ⁡ ( ψ lo ) J(\psi_{\mathrm{hi}})>J(\psi_{\mathrm{lo}}) ;
and totality of V V is used exactly once, to ensure the constructed program halts.
This is the Rice-style phenomenon ( Rice, 1953 ) in relational form; the specificity of J J in Theorem 1 is for concreteness only.
